 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and the resolution of the feature maps. Alpha-blending is $O(N)$ per pixel. Split-sum approximation reduces the complexity of the specular integral from Monte Carlo sampling to texture lookups [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: Requires storage for the feature maps (albedo, metallic, roughness, normal) and the pre-computed lookup textures and cubemaps [S1, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling**: Scales with the number of Gaussians and the resolution of the feature maps [G].
-- **Strengths**: Stabilizes optimization by treating alpha-blending as a smoothing filter. Produces cohesive rendering results. Efficient real-time rendering via split-sum approximation [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Requires pre-computation of lookup textures and cubemaps. Inter-reflection is not fully modeled in the base method (though an extension is mentioned) [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use**: When relightable, physically-based rendering of 3D Gaussians is required, and optimization stability is a concern [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not**: When real-time performance is critical and pre-computation is not feasible, or when inter-reflection is a primary requirement without the extension [G].
